notes               = "One poem running across two printed pages: the dedication and the opening are on 133, the date on 134. The dedication به محمد علی نواب ('to Mohammad Ali Navvab') is printed in the outer margin and is not poem body. Metrical and monorhymed on -ān throughout (آسمان / باران / جوشان / ریحان / جان / برگردان / سرگردان), one of the formal poems clustering after fire-water. Line 14's لای is read as 'fold, layer' but the reading is not certain and the line is kept verbatim. هیهات is the classical cry of unbridgeable distance. 31 Farvardin = 20 April 1991. Imported as a draft with machine / lantern layers; ===translation=== left for Ben. Photo check 2026-07-31 (p133): یخچال روحِ زنجره‌ای is printed as transcribed (روح, with the kasra) — the icehouse of the cicada's soul is the poet's own."
draft               = true

===persian===

شب‌ها
وقتی به یاد دهکده می‌افتم
دیوار سیمانی در خواب کاهگل
و سقف بی پرستو در نقشِ آسمان
بی ابر بی‌ستاره.
یخچالِ روح زنجره‌ای در باران
با خواندنی مَقطع و محزون
شب‌ها
وقتی به یاد دهکده می‌افتم
یک شیر نیم‌بسته چشمهٔ جوشان
و از همه عجیب تر
یک ساق نیم‌خوردهٔ ریحان
که عطر آن با یک نفس
به عمق هزاران هزار لای
تا انتهای جان.
ای آن
به ساحتِ معطر باران
بر بال‌های پرطراوت باران
جسم مرا به دهکده برگردان.
همزادِ شادِ کودکیم تنهاست
در آب‌های تشنهٔ سرگردان.
شب‌ها
وقتی به یاد دهکده می‌افتم
آغوش و نان نرم و تنور گرم
می‌خوانمت دوباره تو را ای جان
هیهات صبح و بویِ خوشِ خشخاش
هیهات خاک و زمزمهٔ باران
۱۳۷۰/۱/۳۱

===machine===

Nights
when the village comes back to me —
a cement wall dreaming it is adobe
and a swallowless roof in the figure of the sky
cloudless, starless.
The icehouse of the soul, a cicada in the rain
with its broken and mournful singing.
Nights
when the village comes back to me —
a half-closed tap, a spring at the boil,
and strangest of all
a half-eaten stalk of basil
whose scent, on one breath,
goes down through thousands upon thousands of folds
to the very end of the soul.
O you —
in the perfumed precinct of the rain,
on the fresh-feathered wings of the rain,
carry my body back to the village.
The glad twin of my childhood is alone
in the thirsty wandering waters.
Nights
when the village comes back to me —
an embrace, and soft bread, and a warm oven —
I call you again, I call you, O life.
How far off the morning and the sweet smell of poppy,
how far off the earth and the murmuring of the rain.
1370/1/31

===lantern===

Nights,
when the village comes back to me:
a cement wall dreaming it is adobe,
a swallowless roof in the shape of the sky,
cloudless, starless.
The icehouse of the soul — a cicada in the rain
with its broken mournful singing.
Nights,
when the village comes back to me:
a half-closed tap, a spring at the boil,
and strangest of all
a half-eaten stalk of basil
whose scent goes down on one breath
through thousands upon thousands of folds
to the very end of the soul.
O you —
in the perfumed precinct of the rain,
on the fresh-feathered wings of the rain,
carry my body back to the village.
The glad twin of my childhood is alone
in the thirsty wandering waters.
Nights,
when the village comes back to me:
an embrace, soft bread, a warm oven —
I call you again, I call you, O life.
How far off the morning and the sweet smell of poppy.
How far off the earth and the murmuring of the rain.

1370/1/31

===translation===

===footnotes===

One poem across two printed pages; the page break falls after *the village comes back to
me*, which is also the line the poem keeps returning to, so the break and the refrain land together.

هیهات, twice in the last two lines, is a classical exclamation with no real English equivalent — it
means both *alas* and *how impossibly far*. 'How far off' takes the second sense because the whole
poem is about distance from a place rather than grief at it.
